# The known divergences: the cases where the port differs from the Go
# compiler on purpose, because the Go compiler is wrong there and the
# port does not copy its bug. Each entry is four sections, and an entry
# of emit-header may have a fifth, all named <id>/<section>:
#
#   divergence  the case, as suite: and case:, the stage whose case it is
#               and its subtest name (suite emit-probes for the programs
#               of testdata/emit-probes), go:, what the Go compiler gets
#               wrong, and why:, what the port does instead and what
#               ruled it, with its date, and same:, none, or the streams,
#               of exit, stdout and stderr, separated by ", ", that are
#               not recorded, where the port must give what the Go
#               compiler gives; a field goes on over the lines after it
#               that start with two spaces
#   exit        the port's exit status, or nothing when same names exit
#   stdout      the port's standard output, as reviewed, or nothing when
#               same names stdout
#   stderr      the port's standard error, as reviewed, or nothing when
#               same names stderr
#   file        for a case of emit-header alone, and only then may same
#               name every stream: path: and the path of a file the
#               case's copy holds afterwards, then its mode and bytes as
#               the port writes them, as reviewed, compared in place of
#               Go's file there
#
# The suite header-render is TestHeaderRender's: its entries record, as
# stdout, the tack.h the port's --port-header must write.
#
# The port is compared with the recorded output in place of the Go
# compiler's, and the case fails when the Go compiler gives the recorded
# output, when the port declines it, and when its suite has no such case
# (see the package doc, Known divergences).
-- kw-struct/divergence --
suite: check-backend
case: invalid/extern-keyword-names/main.tck
go: Go's claimStruct reports a struct whose tack.h name is a C keyword as
  "type T", naming no package as tack.h's other messages about a type
  do, and claims no name for it.
why: The port names it "type T of package P" and claims its name, so a
  later clash with it is reported as with any claimed name, decided by
  the language owner 2026-11-15.
same: none
-- kw-struct/exit --
1
-- kw-struct/stdout --
-- kw-struct/stderr --

